{{Quote|The Holy City was a sight to behold. A beacon of faith for the entire galaxy. Sacred to&hellip; well, sacred to about everyone. Above all, Jedha was a haven. A sanctuary. It was a place to retreat. To reflect and renew. A place where you could discover who you truly were&hellip; and who you would become.|[[Tey Sirrek]]|<ref name="The High Republic 1">[[The High Republic (2022) 1|''The High Republic'' (2022) 1]]</ref>}}
'''Jedha City''', also known as the '''Holy City''', '''NiJedha''' in the ancient texts, or simply '''Jedha''', was an ancient walled [[city]] located on the [[desert]] [[moon]] of [[Jedha]]. In the city was the ancient [[Temple of the Kyber]], a great temple held sacred by those who followed the teachings of the [[Church of the Force]]. For some time before the [[Battle of Yavin]], the [[Galactic Empire]] had occupied the city, an action that was met with firm [[Jedha insurgency|resistance]] by [[Saw Gerrera]]'s [[Partisans]]. Jedha City was destroyed by a single-reactor [[superlaser]] blast from the newly-operational [[DS-1 Orbital Battle Station|Death Star]].

==Description==
{{Quote|And as Aida keeps reminding me: there's no better place than the Holy City to find peace.|[[Creighton Sun]]|<ref name="Battle">''[[The High Republic: The Battle of Jedha]]''</ref>}}
[[File:Streets_of_Jedha.png|thumb|left|245px|The streets of Jedha City, patrolled by Imperial personnel]]
Jedha was an ancient [[city]] located on the<ref name="Rogue One" /> [[Temperature|cold]], [[desert]]<ref name="Jedha DB">{{Databank|jedha|Jedha}}</ref> [[Mid Rim Territories|Mid Rim]]<ref name="Visual Guide" /> [[moon]] of [[Jedha]]. Standing atop a rocky [[mesa]],<ref name="Galactic Atlas" /> the city was surrounded by a large wall and contained various temples and adorned buildings.<ref name="Rogue One" /> The city was divided into two sections by a [[Division Wall]], one section was known as the Old City and the other known as the New City, though even the New City was well over [[5000 BBY|5000 years old]].<ref name="Guardians of the Whills" /> The most notable structure in the Holy City was the ancient [[Temple of the Kyber]]&mdash;a great temple protruding high above the city wall that was held sacred by those who followed the teachings of the [[Church of the Force]].<ref name="Galactic Atlas" />

Considered a holy site for followers of the mystical energy field known as [[the Force]], the Holy City's streets were filled with [[priest]]s, [[scholar]]s, and holy [[Human|men]] and [[Gender|women]],<ref name="EW Jedha">{{WebCite|url=http://www.ew.com/article/2016/08/08/star-wars-rogue-one-force-sacred-world-jedha-details|text=''Rogue One'' director reveals new details on Force-sacred world Jedha|author=Breznican, Anthony|date=2016-08-08|work=Entertainment Weekly|archivedate=20160809143943}}</ref> many of them [[pilgrim]]s, who could be recognized by their distinctive and often ornate robes.<ref name="Galactic Atlas" />

==History==
===High Republic Era===
{{Quote|Jedi aren't always the most welcome on Jedha.''"<br />"''Not welcome? In the ''Holy City?'' But we&mdash;''"<br />"''Used to rule the place?''"<br />"''I wouldn't say rule.''"<br />"''Govern it, then. A long time ago, sure, but folks around here have even longer memories.|[[Matthea Cathley]] and [[Vildar Mac]]|<ref name="The High Republic 1" />}}
[[File:BattleofJedha-2022THRA4.png|thumb|right|250px|The Holy City finds itself at war in 382 BBY in part due to lingering tensions between Jedha's religions.]]
Long before<ref name="The High Republic 1" /> [[382 BBY]],<ref name="Peace and Unity date">{{PeaceandUnityDate}}</ref> the city was governed by the [[Jedi Order]]. During this time, four statues of [[Jedi]] were built in the [[desert]]s outside the city, though by<ref name="Peace and Unity">{{StoryCite|story=Peace and Unity|book=The High Republic (2022) 1}}</ref> 382 BBY,<ref name="Peace and Unity date" /> only one of the statues,<ref name="Peace and Unity" /> known as the [[Final Protector]],<ref name="The High Republic 5">[[The High Republic (2022) 5|''The High Republic'' (2022) 5]]</ref> still stood.<ref name="Peace and Unity" /> The Jedi Order's control over Jedha City, regarded by some as a "rule" over the Holy City, generated dislike towards the Order:<ref name="The High Republic 1" /> the Order eventually stepped back when the other religions objected to Jedi control, as those faiths feared the Jedi were trying to position themselves as the one true religion.<ref name="Path Ven">''[[The High Republic: Path of Vengeance]]''</ref>

Anti-Jedi sentiment remained to a degree in the Holy City among some citizens.<ref name="The High Republic 1" /> The [[Battle of Jedha]] instigated by the [[Path of the Open Hand]] saw fighting hit the city, with the Final Protector falling during the battle<ref name="Battle of Jedha" /> at the hands of anti-Jedi protestors. The battle was generated in part by religious tensions in the city, including lingering anti-Jedi sentiment. The battle also involved the forces of [[Eiram's military]] and [[E'ronoh's military]] clashing in the Holy City,<ref name="Path Ven" /> where they had hoped to sign a [[Summit on Jedha|peace treaty]] interrupted by the Path's scheme.<ref name="Battle of Jedha" /> The rioting people of Jedha also clashed with the [[soldier]]s suddenly before them. After the battle, while the peace that fell over the city was uneasy, various factions came together in the name of rebuilding Jedha City.<ref name="Path Ven" />

===Imperial Era===
[[File:Acme-StreetsofJedha.png|thumb|left|250px|Imperial forces took control of the holy city]]
In the wake of the [[Jedi Order]]'s [[Great Jedi Purge|eradication]] and the [[Declaration of a New Order]] by the [[Galactic Empire]]<ref name="RotS">{{Film|III}}</ref> in [[19 BBY]],<ref name="MoFDate">{{MOFdate|1}}</ref> a procession of mourners gathered in the Holy City to grieve the fallen [[Jedi]]. Cries mourning Jedi [[Saesee Tiin]], [[Stass Allie]], [[O'ra've]] and [[Caladastorous]] were heard. Among the attendees was [[Senator]] [[Bail Prestor Organa]], traveling incognito in [[Ztenortha pilgrim]] clothing, and a [[Tarsunt]] admiral acquainted with Organa.<ref name="MoF">''[[Reign of the Empire: The Mask of Fear]]''</ref>

Amidst the somber vigil, in which mourners pushed the massive rotating disk known as the [[Stone of First Tears]] in ritual mourning, a [[Bombing in the Holy City|bomb detonated]] within the city, triggering chaos and widespread panic. The detonation caused a stampede, in which several individuals were crushed as they attempted to flee through narrow streets. Later, news feeds described the perpetrator as aligned ideologically with the Imperial regime, wishing to eliminate the Jedi and their supporters.<ref name="MoF" />

Sometime before the [[Battle of Scarif]],<ref name="Galactic Atlas" /> Jedha City, along with the rest of the moon, was occupied by the [[Galactic Empire]], with Imperial [[stormtrooper]]s, [[KX-series security droid]], [[TX-225 GAVw "Occupier" combat assault tank]]s, [[TIE/ln space superiority starfighter]]s, and [[Zeta-class Heavy Cargo Shuttle|cargo shuttles]] being deployed to the city. However, the occupation was met with firm [[Jedha insurgency|resistance]] by rebel extremist [[Saw Gerrera]]<ref name="Rogue One" />&mdash;leader of the [[Partisans]]<ref name="Bloodline">[[Bloodline (novel)|''Bloodline'']]</ref>&mdash;who coordinated the insurgency from his [[Catacombs of Cadera|hideout]].<ref name="Galactic Atlas" /> In response to Gerrera's insurgency, the [[Imperial I-class Star Destroyer|''Imperial I''-class Star Destroyer]] ''[[Dauntless (Imperial I-class)|Dauntless]]'' was dispatched to sit over Jedha City<ref name="Rogue One" /> at the suggestion of [[Imperial Intelligence]].<ref name="197 Probe">{{InsiderCite|197|Probing the Galaxy: Imperial Reconnaissance Report}}</ref>

A [[Battle of Jedha City]] was fought<ref name="GotW">[[Guardians of the Whills (novel)|''Guardians of the Whills'']]</ref> in [[1 BBY]].<ref name="Timelines">''[[Star Wars: Timelines]]''</ref> During the [[Alliance to Restore the Republic]]'s mission to find the location of Imperial [[scientist]] [[Galen Walton Erso|Galen Erso]], [[Captain]] [[Cassian Jeron Andor|Cassian Andor]], [[Jyn Erso]], and reprogrammed Imperial [[KX-series security droid]] [[K-2SO]] [[Battle on Jedha|traveled]] to the Holy City to meet with Gerrera. While there, the trio became involved in a firefight with Imperial and Partisan forces.<ref name="Rogue One" />

[[File:MeltingJedhaCity-RO.png|thumb|right|250px|The Death Star firing on Jedha City]]
[[Standard hour|Hours]] later, all Imperial forces were [[Protocol 13|ordered to withdraw]] from the city immediately. Unknown to many of them, [[Director]] [[Orson Callan Krennic|Orson Krennic]] and [[Grand Moff]] [[Wilhuff Tarkin]] had decided to test the capability of the Empire's new [[superweapon]]-equipped [[battle station]], the [[DS-1 Orbital Battle Station|Death Star]], by firing its [[superlaser]] at the city using a single-[[reactor]] ignition. The test was a success, resulting in the complete [[Destruction of Jedha City|destruction]] of the Holy City and its inhabitants, as well as the surrounding area.<ref name="Rogue One" /> At least 3% of Jedha's Imperial forces were presumed destroyed in the blast as well, having been unable to evacuate. The Imperial casualties included such forces as [[JN-093's squad]], who had been assigned to check possible rebel hiding spots outside the city, leaving them unable to get to the extraction point in time.<ref name="Rogue One novel" />

===After the blast===
[[File:Jedha_city_crater.jpg|thumb|left|The "crater" where Jedha City once stood]]
In order to prevent the public from finding out about the weapon and the true circumstances of Jedha City's destruction, the Empire portrayed the event as a [[mining]] catastrophe to the [[Imperial Senate]].<ref name="Rogue One" /> While some reports indicated that the city's destruction had left only a modest crater,<ref name="Rebel Files">''[[Star Wars: The Rebel Files]]''</ref> in reality the Death Star's blast had obliterated almost a quarter of the moon and exposed its core. Alliance forces traveled to the ruins of Jedha to meet with the remaining Partisans<ref name="Star Wars 38">[[Star Wars (2015) 38|''Star Wars'' (2015) 38]]</ref> in [[1 ABY]].<ref name="Timelines" />

==Behind the scenes==
{{Quote|I had a luck to live and work in Sarajevo during 2008, and in that period I made a lot of pictures of the City. Sarajevo is a melting pot of cultures, and during my work on one of the scenes from Jedha, it occurred to me that two cities had a similar vibe. Photos that I used, I took at a hill facing Baščaršija and the Old Town.|translated=1|Michele Benigna|<ref>{{WebCite|url=http://www.radiosarajevo.ba/metromahala/lica/kako-su-se-prizori-sarajeva-nasli-u-novom-star-wars-filmu/250930|text=Kako su se prizori Sarajeva našli u novom Star Wars filmu |date=2017-01-16|archivedate=20230523005704}}</ref>}}
Jedha City is a location created and developed for the [[2016]] ''[[Star Wars]]'' [[Star Wars Anthology Series|anthology film]], ''[[Rogue One: A Star Wars Story]]''. The city was inspired by real life [[Wikipedia:Holy city|holy cities]] such as [[Wikipedia:Mecca|Mecca]] and [[Wikipedia:Jerusalem|Jerusalem]] and has [[Wikipedia:Middle East|Middle Eastern]] influences with its narrow market alleyways, desert sands, and [[Wikipedia:Ottoman architecture|Ottoman-inspired architecture]].<ref name="EW Jedha" /> It is also partly inspired by [[Wikipedia:Sarajevo|Sarajevo]], a city situated on the [[Wikipedia:Balkans|Balkans]] and usually referred to as Jerusalem of Europe, due to its multicultural nature. Jedha City also bears striking similarity with the Jewish fortress-city of [[Wikipedia:Masada|Masada]]. First footage of the holy city was released with the teaser trailer for ''Rogue One: A Star Wars Story'' on [[April 7]], [[2016]].<ref name="Teaser">{{SWYouTube|2BDyeARyIkw|Rogue One: A Star Wars Story Trailer Tease}}</ref> The city was first identified as the holy city in ''[[Star Wars: Galactic Atlas]]'', a reference book released on [[November 3]], 2016.<ref name="Galactic Atlas" />

